\title{The subspace-intersection inequality}
\taxon{Lemma}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{[Constrained spaces](cn-0019); [Interpolation identity](cn-0020); [Adapted columns](cn-0078); [Degree budget](cn-0023); [Sharpness construction](cn-0025); [Diagonal disjointness](cn-0076)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Lemma 2.1, equation (2.1), and Remark 2.2; reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026, with sharpness made explicit in the statement on 11 October 2026. [Lean source for the inequality: SubspaceIntersection](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/SubspaceIntersection.lean).}

\p{\strong{Statement.} Let #{W} be a real vector space of dimension #{N}, and let #{U_i,V_i\subseteq W}, #{1\leq i\leq m}, be subspaces with #{m\geq1} and #{U_i\cap V_i=0}, that is, with [diagonal disjointness](cn-0076). Then}

##{\sum_{i,j=1}^m\dim(U_i\cap V_j)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor N.}

\p{This bound is sharp: for every #{m\geq1} and #{N\geq0}, there are such families attaining equality.}

\p{\strong{Proof.} Choose distinct nodes and use [the constrained spaces](cn-0019). Fix a non-node #{t_*}. In the [stagewise adapted family](cn-0078), #{\ell_e=k_e(W)-k_{e-1}(W)} is the number of columns introduced at stage #{e}. Sum [the degree budget](cn-0023) over #{0\leq h\leq m-1}. By [the interpolation identity](cn-0020),}

##{\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i,j}\dim(U_i\cap V_j)
&=\sum_{h=0}^{m-1}\sum_j k_h(V_j)\\
&\leq\sum_{h=0}^{m-1}\sum_{e=0}^h e\ell_e
=\sum_{e=0}^{m-1}e(m-e)\ell_e.
\end{aligned}}

\p{A column introduced at stage #{e} contributes its degree bound #{e} at each of the #{m-e} stages #{h=e,\ldots,m-1}. This explains the factor #{e(m-e)} in the last sum. Since #{e(m-e)\leq\lfloor m^2/4\rfloor}, #{\ell_e\geq0}, and}

##{\sum_{e=0}^{m-1}\ell_e=k_{m-1}(W)=\dim E_{m-1}(W;t_*)\leq N,}

\p{the claimed bound follows. Sharpness follows from [the construction in cn-0025](cn-0025), which attains equality for every #{m} and #{N}. The paper compares the determinant-multiplicity method with [subspace designs](cn-0050), but the proof here supplies its own argument for these arbitrary subspaces.}

\p{The real field matches the paper's statement. [The field-of-scalars remark](cn-0065) proves that this algebraic inequality holds over every field.}

\p{[A short counting proof](cn-0073) handles subspaces spanned by subsets of one common basis. [The comparison with Scott and Wilmer](cn-0072) examines whether exterior-algebra methods can replace the general proof; [a projection argument](cn-0074) refines #{N} to #{\max_i(\dim U_i+\dim V_i)}.}
